WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.229 --> 00:00:12.210
- Hey everyone, my name is Jesse

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.300
from the Buzzfeed Food team,

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.970
and today, I'm gonna
be testing five famous

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.040
celebrity guacamole
recipes to see which one

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.400
is actually the best.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.600
Today, I'll be testing
recipes from Rachael Ray,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.340
Ayesha Curry, Alton Brown,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.700
Marcella Valladolid, and Beyonce.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.360
I measured all the ingredients precisely

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.360
as the recipe stated to
level the playing field,

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:29.470
and I got three of my friends

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.740
to try our guacamole
in a blind taste test.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.920
I picked my winner, but I
wanna see what they thought.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.420
So, game on.
(whistle blows)

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.920
Rachael Ray starts by
mixing diced red onions

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.240
with lemon juice and salt, mixes it up,

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.300
and let's it sit for about 10 minutes.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.630
After that, she cuts
and pits two avocados,

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:47.570
and scoops the flesh out on
top of that onion mixture,

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.270
then adds some cilantro,
jalapeno and garlic,

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.090
and mashes it all together.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.350
But, here's where things get crazy.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.370
Next, she adds even more lemon juice,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.050
diced apples, and pureed
chipotle in adobo,

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:01.300
then mixes that all up and seasons it.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.650
To finish it, she
transfers some into a bowl,

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.650
and tops it liberally and
entirely with toasted almonds

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.100
and even more apples.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.070
- We got almonds on the
guacamole, that's not a thing.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.900
- Ooh, okay, okay, very chunky.

00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:16.280
- If I could think of,
like, Hawaiian guacamole,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.570
this is what I think I'd be getting.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:21.160
- Guacamole usually has
big texture problems,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.630
so I don't mind the little bit of crunch

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:23.640
from the almonds.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.840
- The almonds are good,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.090
they kinda break up the fruitiness

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.690
and make it a little more savory.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.060
- I'm kinda freaking out about this one

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.530
'cause I don't know what to make of it,

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:31.660
but I do like it.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:32.570
- I don't love it.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:33.530
- I like it.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.730
It's different, but if we
go around not liking things

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.160
that are different from us, come on, man,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:40.580
America, wake up.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.510
- [Jesse] Ayesha Curry starts
by scooping the meat out

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.000
of a single avocado and
placing it into a bowl.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.370
She called it meat, not me,

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.500
so don't @ me in the
comments for this one.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.090
She then adds diced shallots,

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.120
hot sauce, chunky garlic paste,

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.230
lime juice, salt and pepper,

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.050
then just mashes that all up with a fork

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.400
and that's it for this one.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.590
- Looks like this was a project

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:02.870
between a mom and a child,

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.320
and this is all they could achieve.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.820
- If something slaps me
across my taste buds,

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.570
I'm gonna be angry because
it is so unassuming-looking.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.650
- This is definitely,
yeah, like a bar-quality.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.760
- Nothing special, it's fine.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:18.593
- That's very good.

00:02:18.593 --> 00:02:22.020
You know, it's not spicy,
but it's very creamy.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.520
Just your basic guacamole, but it's good.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.440
I like this one.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:25.840
- [Jesse] Up next it Alton Brown.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.060
He starts with three whole avocados,

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.470
cuts them in half, takes the pit out,

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.340
and scoops the flesh into a bowl.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.010
To that, he adds lime juice,

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.620
tosses it all together, and
strains out the extra juice

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.000
into a tiny bowl 'cause
you'll use this later.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.090
Next, he adds cayenne and cumin,

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:43.010
and mashes it all together
with a potato masher

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.410
to give it a nice, creamy texture.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:48.200
He adds garlic, onions, jalapeno,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.210
cilantro, tomatoes, and
the reserved lime juice,

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.910
and mixes that all together.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.120
And, that's it for this
one, super classic.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.525
- This looks like a classic guacamole.

00:02:59.525 --> 00:03:02.060
The colors here say, "Viva Mexico."

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.400
- This looks like the type of guac

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.250
that you get when you
order it at a restaurant.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.070
- It's not very guacamole-y-y.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.020
- There's an issue here.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.060
There is a lack of flavor.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.640
- It almost doesn't taste like avocado

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.234
or like vegetables, it
tastes like taco seasoning.

00:03:19.234 --> 00:03:20.880
- There's not enough seasoning in here,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.000
not enough salt.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.382
The basic

00:03:23.382 --> 00:03:24.324
of all

00:03:24.324 --> 00:03:25.157
guac.

00:03:25.157 --> 00:03:26.720
- I'll say I don't like this one.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.620
It presents itself in a way

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:29.860
where it looks like it's gonna be

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.390
the ultimate guacamole, and then it isn't.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.540
- [Jesse] Up next is Food
Network personality, Marcela V,

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.510
just 'cause I don't wanna
butcher her last name,

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:39.770
with her sweet and spicy guacamole.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.200
She starts by cutting open five avocados,

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.450
pits them, and roughly
mashes them all together

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.820
with a fork to give it a nice texture.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.200
To that, she adds diced mango,

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.470
red pepper flakes, a
generous amount of salt,

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.040
and a ton of lime juice,
and mixes it all together.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.470
She places it into a bowl

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.980
and tops it with chili
oil to add a pop of heat.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.880
- It looks beautiful, it's attractive.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.690
- This looks like it's gonna be spicy.

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:05.250
- There's a big chunk of an orange fruit,

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.180
really throwing me for a loop here.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.150
- Oh, I lost sense of time.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.240
Where am I?

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.720
- What is that?

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.240
It tastes like Chinese food oil.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.640
- Tastes almost, like, Asian-infused.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.500
- This one is the most
interesting by far, and it's good.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.333
I'm into it.

00:04:24.333 --> 00:04:25.820
- [Jesse] And last is
the Queen Bee herself

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.030
with a super traditional recipe for guac.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.830
She starts by peeling two avocados

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.750
and smashing them together with a spoon

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.100
to smooth it all out.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.040
Next, she adds lime juice, tomato,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.020
diced onion, salt, pepper, and garlic,

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.920
and mixes that all together
until it's all incorporated.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.830
Transfer it to a bowl, and that's it.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.750
- There was another that looked like

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.780
it's about to be a classic experience.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.620
- Picturesque, little tomatoes in there.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.150
- This is what you'll have
at a Mexican restaurant.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:56.940
This has the right amount of seasoning,

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:59.010
the right amount of tomatoes, onions.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.870
- This is almost, like,
the closest to just, like,

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:02.960
guacamole.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.210
By the books, it seems
pretty straightforward.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.440
- The consistency is really nice,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.340
there's some texture to
it, it's not too chunky.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:11.730
It's really good.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:13.850
- [Jesse] Which celebrity has
the best guacamole recipe?

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.120
Let's find out.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.110
- My favorite out of
all these five recipes

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.050
was number four.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.090
A beautiful reinvention
of a classic recipe,

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:24.180
done the freakin' right way.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.000
You got sweet, you got
salty, you got everything.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:29.390
Are you serious?

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:31.270
Marcela, that is awesome.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:32.760
- My favorite was number five.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.680
It was just, like, your typical guacamole,

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.120
but it was very good,
like, a good texture,

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.390
still creamy, there was something to,

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:40.410
like, bite into with the
onions and the tomatoes,

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.173
and I could eat that any day.

00:05:42.173 --> 00:05:44.000
(gasps) Get out.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.460
Blue Ivy is one lucky girl,

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.010
eating that all the time. (laughs)

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.880
- If I had to pick a
favorite guacamole today,

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.890
it is, without question,
guacamole number four

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:55.220
with the oil and the mangoes in there.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.450
I was blown away, it's good guacamole.

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:58.890
I don't know who that is,

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:02.200
but my theoretical hat is off to you.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.040
- [Jesse] There you have it,

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.620
Marcela Valladolid has the best guacamole.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.040
Which celebrity recipe should we try next?

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.640
Hit us up in the comments below.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.320
- It may be even better
than the classic recipe.

